---
/**
 * The config reference, rendered from the release's `docs.json`.
 *
 * bushel derives that file from two places at once — keys and defaults from
 * `Config::default()`, flags and help text from clap's `Args` — and fails its
 * own build when the two disagree, which is what lets this page print a table
 * nobody maintains. The TOML block below is generated from the same options,
 * so it cannot list a key the table does not.
 *
 * Decided in https://github.com/frankieramirez/bushel/issues/47
 */
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { bushelDocs } from "../../lib/bushelDocs";

const { version, config } = await bushelDocs();

/** TOML for the defaults, in the order bushel declares its flags. */
const example = config.options
  .map((option) => `${option.key} = ${JSON.stringify(option.default)}`)
  .join("\n");

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "options", text: "Options" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "layouts-and-settings", text: "Layouts and settings" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "flags-and-the-file", text: "Flags and the file" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "config-directory", text: "Config directory" },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Config"
  description="bushel's config file options, their defaults, and the flags that set the same things."
  lede="Four options, a file that holds them, and the flags that set the same things."
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    bushel reads <code>{config.path}</code>, which holds the same options the
    command-line flags set. The file is optional and every key below is optional
    inside it. Set <code>BUSHEL_CONFIG_DIR</code> to move the directory that
    contains <code>config.toml</code>. This page is generated from bushel v{version},
    so the defaults are the ones the binary actually starts with.
  </p>

  <p>Writing out the defaults gives you a file that changes nothing:</p>

  <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="options">Options</h2>

  <div class="docs-scroll">
    <table class="docs-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Flag</th>
          <th>Default</th>
          <th>Does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          config.options.map((option) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>{option.key}</code>
              </td>
              <td>
                <code>{option.flag}</code>
              </td>
              <td>
                <code>{JSON.stringify(option.default)}</code>
              </td>
              <td>{option.desc}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="layouts-and-settings">Layouts and settings</h2>

  <p>
    <code>rail</code> is the default: all four panes remain visible, with the
    active one expanded beside the detail pane. In narrow terminals the rail
    moves above the detail. <code>table</code> puts one full-width table above
    one full-width detail pane at every size. Both use the same pane and action keys.
  </p>

  <pre><code>bushel --layout table</code></pre>

  <p>
    Press <code>,</code> to open settings. Move between rows with
    <code>j</code>/<code>k</code> or the up/down arrows, then press
    <code>enter</code> or <code>space</code> to change the selected setting.
    Changes apply immediately and are saved for future launches. Close the panel
    with <code>esc</code>, <code>q</code> or <code>,</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="flags-and-the-file">Flags and the file</h2>

  <p>
    The three boolean flags, <code>--no-splash</code>,
    <code>--reduced-motion</code>, and <code>--ascii</code>, are ORed with the
    values in the file at startup. A true value in the file stays true when you
    omit its flag. Edit the file or use the settings panel to turn it off.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>--layout</code> takes <code>rail</code> or <code>table</code> and
    replaces the file's layout for that run. For example,
    <code>bushel --layout table</code> uses the full-width table without changing
    <code>layout = "rail"</code> in the file. Changing a setting in the panel
    saves that choice while keeping the other saved option values. For example,
    launching with <code>--ascii</code> and changing the layout does not save
    the temporary ASCII override. Saving rewrites the config file, so comments
    and unknown keys are not preserved.
  </p>

  <p>
    A key bushel does not recognise is ignored in silence, so a misspelling like
    <code>reduce_motion</code> is silently dead. A value of the
    wrong type is louder: bushel prints one line naming the file and falls back
    to defaults for all of it.
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#settings-that-do-nothing">Troubleshooting</a>
    has both.
  </p>

  <h2 id="config-directory">Config directory</h2>

  <p>
    <code>BUSHEL_CONFIG_DIR</code> changes the directory used for
    <code>config.toml</code> and the <code>.launched</code> first-run marker.
    For example, this keeps a separate set of preferences:
  </p>

  <pre><code>BUSHEL_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.config/bushel-work" bushel</code></pre>

  <p>
    The settings panel shows the path it saves to. This override does not move
    the shell installer's update receipt; that receipt uses
    <code>XDG_CONFIG_HOME</code>. If a save fails, the change still applies in
    the current session and the message log records the error.
  </p>
</Docs>
